Trace:

  1. _redeemShares is internal and only reachable through the vault's redemption entry point; sharesToRedeem and
  isEscrowed are supplied by the base AbstractYieldStrategy flow, not by the caller directly. isEscrowed is only true
  when the account actually has a pending withdraw request, and sharesToRedeem is bounded by the account's share balance
  in the base contract.
  2. The claimed amplification needs sharesToRedeem > w.sharesAmount. w.sharesAmount was written by _initiateWithdraw,
  which passes sharesAmount: sharesHeld — the account's entire share balance at request time, not a partial amount. So
  immediately after the request, sharesToRedeem <= w.sharesAmount holds by construction. The attacker must therefore
  grow the balance while the request is pending. The two routes named in the claim are (a) depositing again and (b)
  receiving shares — neither is shown in this file to be permitted, and the base strategy's mint path is exactly where
  the "cannot enter position with a pending withdraw request" check lives (the same _isWithdrawRequestPending predicate
  used above in convertToAssets); transfers of shares against a pending request are handled by the manager's
  tokenization path (tokenizeWithdrawRequest), which moves a proportional slice of the request rather than leaving
  w.sharesAmount stale. The candidate names no actual unguarded call site that increases the balance while a request is
  frozen.
  3. Even granting an inflated sharesToRedeem, the computed yieldTokensBurned is not self-executing: it is handed to
  withdrawRequestManager.finalizeAndRedeemWithdrawRequest({withdrawYieldTokenAmount, sharesToBurn}), which is the
  component that owns the shared per-vault cooldown position and the per-account request record. That manager
  independently decrements the stored request (sharesAmount/yieldTokenAmount) and would revert on underflow if asked to
  burn more than the record holds. Nothing in the shown code proves the manager accepts an over-sized
  withdrawYieldTokenAmount; the manager source is not provided at all, so the decisive step of the claimed exploit — the
  escrowed-asset release exceeding what was escrowed — is unverified.
  4. The claim also does not identify how the extra shares acquired after the request would even be classified as
  escrowed: convertToAssets/the redemption router derive escrow status from the request, and the value function returns
  the request's value for the queried shares via getWithdrawRequestValue, again delegating the bound to the manager.

  The exploit rests on an assumed absence of a bound inside code that is not shown, and on a balance-growth path that
  the strategy's pending-request predicate exists precisely to block. Under the tie-break, this cannot survive.

  CONSTRAINT|class=C21|A claim that a stored snapshot quantity can be outgrown by a later-supplied amount must identify
  the concrete unprivileged call path that increases the tracked quantity while the snapshot is frozen, and must show
  the downstream accounting component that actually settles the transfer does not independently re-bound the amount
  against its own stored record.
  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/staking/AbstractStakingStrategy.sol:_redeemShares:100|class=C21|The request is created with the
  account's full share balance (sharesAmount: sharesHeld) and the pending-request predicate gates re-entry into the
